<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
    <!-- See https://aka.ms/maui-publish-app-store#add-entitlements for more information about adding entitlements.-->
    <dict>
        <!--
            App Sandbox is DELIBERATELY DISABLED (REQ-FN-016 / REQ-FN-028).

            The sandbox is only required to distribute through the Mac App Store. TrSetup is a
            developer environment doctor distributed directly, and its entire purpose is to inspect
            and repair the HOST system: shell `dotnet` / `tar` / installers, write the managed PATH
            block into ~/.zprofile, and install tools under ~/.trsetup.

            With the sandbox ON, macOS silently redirects the app's writes into
            ~/Library/Containers/com.techierathore.trsetup/Data/ and blocks execution of downloaded
            binaries. Observed 2026-07-20: clicking Fix on the .NET SDK row downloaded
            dotnet-install.sh into the CONTAINER and nothing ever reached the system, and the app
            kept its own private settings.json divorced from ~/.trsetup/settings.json. Every fixer
            appeared to do nothing. A sandboxed system-repair tool is a contradiction.

            If a Mac App Store build is ever wanted it needs a different target/entitlements set —
            the fixers cannot work under the sandbox regardless.
        -->
        <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
        <false/>
        <!-- Outgoing connections: installer downloads, endpoint probes, NuGet feed checks. -->
        <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
        <true/>
    </dict>
</plist>

